## The name

At the ford of the Jabbok, Jacob wrestles through the night with a man who will not give his name
and who, at dawn, cannot overcome him. Jacob will not let him go without a blessing, and the blessing
is a new name: “Your name shall no longer be Jacob, but Israel, for you have striven with God and
with men, and have prevailed” (Genesis 32:22–32; retold in Hosea 12:3–4). Jacob leaves the ford
limping and names the place Peniel, “the face of God”, because he has seen God face to face and
lived. The name is Hebrew *Yisrāʾēl*: *śārâ*, to strive, to contend, to persist, with *ʾēl*, God.

The name came first to a man, for a struggle. It then passed to his children (the twelve tribes, the
“children of Israel”), then to a kingdom (the northern kingdom, destroyed by Assyria in 722 BCE), then,
across two thousand years of dispersion, to a people who had no state and whose identity was carried
by practice, text and argument. The modern state took the name in May 1948, choosing it over other
proposals such as “Judea” and “Zion”. So the word has at least four referents: a man's struggle with
God, a people, an ancient kingdom, and a modern state. A sentence can set one against another.

“Israel is committing genocide against Israel” does exactly that. The state that took the name is
destroying what the name means: the people of the struggle with God. That includes the Jews in whose
name it acts, whose covenant it betrays, and the Palestinians it kills, who on this reading stand in
Jacob's place: wrestling, wounded, refusing to let go. “Jewish how I am” places Jewishness in the
struggle, not in blood. It fits his statement that the enemy is the belief that “their blood
justifies ethnic cleansing for a land promised to them”.

## Zion, and political Zionism

Zion is a hill in Jerusalem, and in the prophets and psalms it stands for the city, the people, and
the place of God's presence (“By the rivers of Babylon, there we sat down, yea, we wept, when we
remembered Zion”, Psalm 137). For most of Jewish history, return to Zion was a messianic hope, left
to God and to the end of days. It was not a political program.

Political Zionism is a nineteenth-century European nationalism. Theodor Herzl, a secular Viennese
journalist shaken by the antisemitism of the Dreyfus affair, published *Der Judenstaat* in 1896 and
convened the First Zionist Congress at Basel in 1897. Its answer to European antisemitism was a
Jewish nation-state, eventually in Palestine. Britain's Balfour Declaration (1917) promised a
“national home for the Jewish people” in a land where Jews were then about a tenth of the
population. The state's founding in 1948 came with the expulsion or flight of about 750,000
Palestinians: the Nakba.

“Death to Zion”, in his words, names this concept: the claim that a people's blood gives it title to
a land and to the removal of those living on it. It does not name the hill, the psalm, or the Jews.
He says so: “Kill that version of judaism. don't kill those jews.”

## The Jewish rejection of political Zionism

Rejecting political Zionism has a long Jewish tradition of its own. It is not something brought in
from outside.

- The Babylonian Talmud (Ketubot 110b–111a) records three oaths. Israel is not to go up to the land
  “as a wall”, meaning en masse and by force. Israel is not to rebel against the nations. The nations
  are not to oppress Israel excessively. The Satmar Rebbe, Joel Teitelbaum, built *Vayoel Moshe* (1961)
  on these oaths: a Jewish state before the messiah is a rebellion against God. Satmar and Neturei
  Karta still teach this.
- Classical Reform Judaism, in the Pittsburgh Platform (1885): “We consider ourselves no longer a
  nation, but a religious community, and therefore expect neither a return to Palestine … nor the
  restoration of any of the laws concerning the Jewish state.”
- The Jewish Labour Bund, the largest Jewish socialist movement in Eastern Europe, stood for
  *doykeit*, “hereness”: the fight for justice where Jews lived, not emigration to a state.
- Martin Buber and Judah Magnes (Brit Shalom, then Ihud) argued from inside the Zionist movement for
  one binational state of equal Arabs and Jews.
- Today Jewish Voice for Peace and IfNotNow organize against the war on Gaza as Jews. Jewish
  scholars of genocide are among those who have named it genocide.

The prophetic core of the tradition sets the standard by which the state is judged: “He has told
you, O man, what is good; and what does the Lord require of you but to do justice, and to love
kindness, and to walk humbly with your God?” (Micah 6:8). Hillel was asked to teach the whole Torah
while standing on one foot. He answered: “What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbor. That is
the whole Torah; the rest is commentary; go and learn” (Shabbat 31a).

## The kabbalah he points to

“Jews not following Kabbalah” refers to the Jewish mystical tradition, above all Isaac Luria's
teaching in sixteenth-century Safed. Before creation, God contracted to make room for the world. The
vessels meant to hold the divine light shattered. Sparks of that light fell and are scattered through
all things and among all peoples. Exile is the condition of the world itself, not only of the Jews.
Redemption is the gathering of the sparks through the right action of each person, repairing the
world. On this teaching redemption is ethical and universal, and taking land cannot bring it.

## The strand he names

The belief he names, blood and promise as title to land and to removal, has a definite history in
religious Zionism:

- After 1967, Rabbi Zvi Yehuda Kook's students founded Gush Emunim, the settler movement that made
  settling the occupied territories a religious commandment.
- Meir Kahane's Kach party called for expelling Arabs. It was barred from the Knesset in 1988 as
  racist and designated a terrorist organization by the United States in 1997 (delisted in 2022).
- Itamar Ben-Gvir, convicted in 2007 of incitement to racism and of supporting Kach, became Israel's
  national security minister. Bezalel Smotrich, finance minister, said in March 2023, after a settler
  rampage, that the Palestinian town of Huwara “needs to be wiped out”.
- On 28 October 2023, opening the ground invasion of Gaza, Prime Minister Netanyahu told the country
  to “remember what Amalek has done to you” (Deuteronomy 25:17–19). The commandment against Amalek
  in 1 Samuel 15:3 is to kill “both man and woman, infant and suckling”. South Africa cited the
  statement in its genocide case at the International Court of Justice. On 26 January 2024 the court
  found the Palestinians' rights under the Genocide Convention plausible and ordered provisional
  measures. In September 2025 the UN Commission of Inquiry found that Israel has committed genocide
  in Gaza, and the International Association of Genocide Scholars resolved the same.

This is the class he means by “the genocidal jews”: Jews who hold this belief and act on it,
selected by the belief, not by birth. His word choice is deliberate.
